   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2b0bf42d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3c per-SAAP pairwise RAAS trends over aging\n",
    "Provide evidence that RAAS variances between groups are equal, allowing for use of standard parametric tests like ANOVA or Student's t-test.\n",
    "\n",
    "Both Bartlett and Levene test H₀: all groups have equal variance.\n",
    "* p > 0.05 → fail to reject → variances look equal → equal-variance tests (Student's t, Dunnett) are justified.\n",
    "* p ≤ 0.05 → reject → variances differ → an equal-variance test may be inappropriate.\n",
    "You want to fail to reject.\n",
    "\n",
    "(A) Per-SAAP —\n",
    "This runs the test separately within each tissue×SAAP (n≈4 per age) and reports the fraction of SAAPs that reject equal variance. You don't read a single p-value; you read the rejection rate:\n",
    "* ~5% rejection → exactly what you'd expect by chance if variances are truly equal everywhere → equal-variance pooling (Student's t / Dunnett) is fine.\n",
    "* Much higher (say 20–40%) → many SAAPs genuinely have unequal variance across ages → consider Welch's t-test (equal_var=False) or Games-Howell instead.\n",
    "Trust the Levene line over Bartlett. Bartlett is very sensitive to non-normality, and with n=4 per group it over-rejects on heavy tails. Levene with center='median' is the robust one. If the two disagree (Bartlett high reject %, Levene near 5%), that's a signature of non-normality, not unequal variance — go with Levene.\n",
    "\n",
    "(B) Pooled / global —\n",
    "This pools every replicate RAAS value into its age group and gives one statistic + p per comparison. Interpretation of the p-value is the same (high = equal spread), but the \"variance\" here is dominated by SAAP-to-SAAP differences, not within-group biological noise. So:\n",
    "\n",
    "It answers \"does the overall RAAS distribution get wider/narrower with age?\" — \n",
    "* It does not validate the per-SAAP test assumption. Don't use (B) to justify the t-test/Dunnett; use (A) for that.\n",
    "* With thousands of pooled points, these tests are over-powered — even a trivial spread difference will give p ≈ 0. So look at the statistic magnitude and the direction (compare group variances directly), not just whether p < 0.05.\n",
    "\n",
    "Bottom line decision rule\n",
    "* Look at (A) Levene reject %. Near 5% → keep your equal-variance Student/Dunnett results as-is.\n",
    "* If it's high → switch the primary test to a variance-robust one (Welch t / Games-Howell) and note it.\n",
    "* Use (B) only as a descriptive statement about global RAAS spread changing with age — report the statistic, don't over-read the tiny p.\n"
   ]
  },
